<div class="main-text" style="text-align: justify;">
    <p><b><i><h1>Strawberry</h1></b><br>

    Strawberries are one of the most popular and widely grown fruits in the UK. They thrive in the <strong>mild and temperate climate</strong> 
    and can be successfully cultivated across the country, from <strong>southern England to Scotland</strong>. While they grow well outdoors, 
    strawberries can also be cultivated in <strong>greenhouses, polytunnels, or containers</strong> to extend the growing season and protect them 
    from unpredictable weather.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Best Growing Conditions</h2></b><br>
    - <strong>Climate:</strong> Prefers mild summers and cool winters. Protect from heavy frost in colder regions.<br>
    - <strong>Soil:</strong> Well-drained, fertile soil with a pH between 5.5 and 6.5 is ideal.<br>
    - <strong>Sunlight:</strong> Requires full sun for optimal fruit production, at least 6 hours per day.<br>
    - <strong>Watering:</strong> Needs regular watering, especially when flowering and fruiting, but avoid waterlogging.<br>
    - <strong>Spacing:</strong> Space plants 30–40 cm apart to allow good airflow and prevent overcrowding.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Common Strawberry Diseases</h2></b><br>
    The current database does not contain any records of strawberry-related diseases.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Tips for Growing Strawberries in the UK</h2></b><br>
    - <strong>Choose the right variety:</strong> ‘Cambridge Favourite’, ‘Honeoye’, and ‘Albion’ are excellent choices for the UK climate.<br>
    - <strong>Mulching:</strong> Use straw, bark, or black plastic to keep fruit off the soil and reduce rot risk.<br>
    - <strong>Fertilizing:</strong> Apply a balanced fertilizer in early spring to encourage strong growth and high yields.<br>
    - <strong>Protect from frost:</strong> In colder areas, cover plants with fleece during winter to prevent damage.<br>
    - <strong>Bird Protection:</strong> Use netting to prevent birds from eating the fruit before harvest.<br><br>

    Strawberries are a rewarding and easy-to-grow crop in the UK, providing fresh, sweet fruit throughout the summer months.
    </i></p>
